    {
      "short": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "title": "Forget me now",
      "body": [
        "<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Sessions kept in one server's memory break as soon as there are two servers.</li><li>Sticky sessions hide the problem, but unbalance load and lose sessions when a server dies.</li><li>Stateless servers keep sessions in a shared store, or in signed tokens the client carries.</li><li>The shared store becomes critical — replicate and monitor it.</li><li>Stateless servers can be added, removed and redeployed at will.</li></ul>"
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "next",
        "text": "Next: keeping popular answers close at hand — caching."
      },
